$ RUN_VALGRIND=0 ./templates/minimal-cuda-ofx/test/run-tests.sh
  == [2/5] PTX instruction counts (nvcc, -arch=sm_89) ==
    vec4  : 1 ld.global.nc.v4.f32 + 1 st.global.v4.f32   (expect 1 + 1)
    scalar: 4 ld.global.nc.f32    + 4 st.global.f32     (expect 4 + 4)
    16F   : 2 ld.global.nc.u32    + 2 st.global.u32      + 8 cvt.f32.f16   (expect 2 + 2 + 8)
    8-bit : 4 ld.global.nc.u8     + 4 st.global.u8       + 4 cvt.rni.s32.f32 (expect 4 + 4 + 4)
  == [3/5] bundle hygiene ==
    nv_fatbin sections:       1 (0 in a CPU-only build)
  contract_test: 162 checks, 0 failed      (nuke / resolve / natron)
  PASS: all cases match
  all checks passed

# What this proves, and what it does not:
#
# [V] both new kernel bodies compile with nvcc 12.8.93 for sm_89 - the real
#     compiler, not test/shim - and appear as entries in the PTX.
# [V] the instruction mix that defines each kernel is what the compiler emits:
#     two 32-bit half2 accesses and eight half<->float conversions for 16F; four
#     byte loads/stores, four multiplies, four cvt.rni (round to nearest even) and
#     the min/max clamp for 8-bit. The suite now asserts all four kernels, so a
#     change that stops the compiler trusting the half2 indexing fails there.
# [V] ptxas allocates them with no spills: 18 registers for 16F, 16 for 8-bit.
# [V] the three launchers compile, link into the bundle together with one
#     nv_fatbin section, and the module still exports exactly the two registry
#     symbols - the version script is not bypassed by the new C entry points.
# [V] the contract harness passes on that CUDA-enabled bundle (162 checks per
#     host profile): the host enables no CUDA, so the plug-in takes its CPU path,
#     which is what those checks were always about.
# [U] nothing ran on a device: this cell has no GPU and no libcuda, so the
#     launchers were compiled and linked but never executed, and no throughput
#     number exists for either depth.
